{"name":"Opalescence","hex":"#b4c0b6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"opalescence","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["the state of being opalescent","the milky iridescent appearance of a dense transparent medium when it is illuminated by polychromatic visible radiation (such as sunlight) due to local fluctuations in its density and therefore in its refractive index"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/opalescence"}],"entries":[{"title":"Opalescence","blocks":[{"text":"Opalescence or play of color is an optical phenomenon associated with the mineraloid gemstone opal, a hydrated silicon dioxide. This effect appears as a milky, translucent glow that changes with the angle of light, often creating a soft, pearly sheen that can display various colors or hues. Opalescence can be seen in materials like certain minerals, glass, and even fluids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Common_Rough_Opal.jpg/330px-Common_Rough_Opal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opalescence","wikidata":"Q1456962"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Opal-53714.jpg/500px-Opal-53714.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Opal-53714.jpg/500px-Opal-53714.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rough sample of precious opal, showing iridescence","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOpal-53714.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Why_is_the_sky_blue.jpg/500px-Why_is_the_sky_blue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Why_is_the_sky_blue.jpg/500px-Why_is_the_sky_blue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tyndall effect in opalescent glass: it appears blue from the side, but orange light shines through.[7]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhy_is_the_sky_blue.jpg"}],"sounds":{"opalescence":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Ne%C3%B8n-opalescence.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/d/db/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Ne%C3%B8n-opalescence.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Ne%C3%B8n-opalescence.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/d/db/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Ne%C3%B8n-opalescence.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Ne%C3%B8n-opalescence.wav.ogg"}]}}}